Original commit body from 13100d6b2b73c49fbde1e6b0d2c91b089cd26a8e:

Two halves of one gap in rust.py's handling of `use`.

The imports edge. The use_declaration branch took the imported path from
the first child of type scoped_identifier / identifier / use_wildcard /
use_list. A brace list (`use crate::error::{A, B};`, a scoped_use_list)
and an alias (`use foo::bar as baz;`, a use_as_clause) matched none, so
the declaration emitted NO imports edge -- among the most common use
forms. When no single path node is there, the use-tree walker the alias
map already uses (_use_tree_bindings: lists, nested lists, `self`,
aliases) now expands every path the declaration binds, and each is
resolved as a single path is (WI-rilof's _InTreeCrates: the module file
in the tree, else the `rust:<path>:0-0:module` stand-in it always was);
one edge per distinct target, so `use crate::m::{a, b};` is one import of
m.rs. A top-level `use { a::b, c };` (just's prelude) matched `use_list` and
emitted ONE edge whose module slot was the whole multi-line brace text; its
items are now walked the same way.

The framework evidence this exposed. refine_frameworks keeps a
manifest-detected framework only if production code imports one of its
modules, and keeps it unvalidated for a language with no imports edges.
With brace-list uses emitting edges, the actix route-parity fixture had
edges, and `actix_web::get` matched nothing: _module_match_kind knew `.`,
`/` and `\` but not Rust's `::`, and Cargo's `actix-web` is imported as
`actix_web`. Both are fixed in profile.py (a `::` prefix arm; a Rust
framework's import names include the crate name with `-` read as `_`).
The `::` gap predates this change: any single-path `use tokio::spawn;` was
already evidence nothing could match.

The call. `use crate_b::{Thing, helper as h2}; h2()` looked up a symbol
NAMED h2, found none, and minted rust:crate_b:0-0:helper -- a
workspace-prefixed stand-in (INV-nuzas). Looking up the target's name
instead is wrong too: it prefers a same-named function of the calling file
(zellij's `fn generate_random_name` wraps the aliased one, and would have
read as calling itself). An aliased call now resolves the alias's PATH
with _rust_function_in_module (#1947's resolver for function values): in
the tree, the function in the module file the path names; outside it,
nothing, leaving the stand-in.

